2 CONT Fatimah bint Asad (c.68 BH – 4 AH ; c.555–626 CE) (Arabic: فاطمة بنت أسد‎, Fāṭimah bint ʾAsad) was the mother of Ali bin Abi Talib, the fourth caliph of Islam. She was the daughter of Asad ibn Hashim and Fatimah bint Qays, hence a
2 CONC  member of the Hashim clan of the Quraysh.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT She married her cousin, Abu Talib ibn Abd al-Muttalib, and was the mother of seven of his children. Talib. Fakhitah (Umm Hani). Aqeel. Jumanah. Ja'far. Rayta. Ali, who was the husband of Muhammad's daughter Fatimah.[2] The orphane
2 CONC d Muhammad, who was Abu Talib's nephew and Fatimah's cousin, came to live in their house in 579 (when he was eight).[3][4] Fatimah became a Muslim and is described as a "righteous woman".[5] Following Abu Talib's death in 620,[6][
2 CONC 7] Fatimah emigrated to Medina with her son Ali in 622.[8] Muhammad used to visit her and take siestas in her house there.[9] She died in the year 625/626.[10] According to Anas bin Malik, when Muhammad learned that Fatimah had di
2 CONC ed, he went to her house to sit beside her body and pray for her,[citation needed] gave his shirt to be incorporated into her shroud, and personally placed her in her grave in the Jannatul Baqee cemetery in Medina.[11]
2 CONT --------------------
