1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Āmina bint Wahb''' (d. 46 before hijra/577), was the mother of the Prophet and one of the most eminent women of Quraysh. Her parents were distant cousins from an illustrious Qurayshī family: her father was Wahb 
2 CONC b. ʿAbd Manāf b. Zuhra b. Kilāb, and her mother Barra bint ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā b. ʿUthmān b. ʿAbd al-Dār b. Quṣayy b. Kilāb. After the death of her father, who had been chief of the Banū Zuhra, she is said to have been under the guardia
2 CONC nship of his successor, her uncle Wuhayb b. ʿAbd Manāf.
2 CONT
2 CONT Āmina married ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib (q.v.) in the year 53 or 54 before hijra/569 or 570. There are various accounts of her marriage: one day ʿAbd Allāh, who some years previously had famously been reprieved by the sacrifi
2 CONC ce of 100 camels, was out hunting on his own, when he was attacked by a group of his enemies: he managed to fight them off and killed a number of them with his bow and arrows. This clash was witnessed by Wahb b. ʿAbd Manāf, who al
2 CONC so saw the bravery and skill in archery that ʿAbd Allāh displayed. Wahb then went to tell the Banū Hāshim, and ʿAbd Allāh’s brothers hurried to the scene, and took the rest of his attackers prisoner. Deeply impressed by this episo
2 CONC de, Wahb b. ʿAbd ¶ Manāf suggested to ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib that his daughter Āmina, who was renowned as one of the most beautiful, virtuous and modest of women, should marry ʿAbd Allāh, to which ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib agreed. ʿAbd Allāh als
2 CONC o consented and the wedding was arranged; Wahb had hoped that his daughter could marry without a dowry, but at ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib’s insistence the dowry was set at 4,000 dirhams. Eventually, a double marriage took place, with ʿAbd a
2 CONC l-Muṭṭalib simultaneously marrying Āmina’s cousin, Hāla (al-Majlisī, 15/94–102, 282; Ibn Saʿd, 1/94–95; Ibn Isḥāq, 100; see also Watt, 1/438; Lings, 17–18).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Ibn Isḥāq (p. 102), Āmina gave birth to Muḥammad ‘on Monday 12 Rabīʿ I in the Year of the Elephant’, which is generally taken to be the year 52 or 53 before hijra/570 or 571. However, this apparently confident dating 
2 CONC is highly doubtful and has often been questioned by later historians and scholars. For example, the exact dating of the ‘Year of the Elephant’ is unclear (see q.v. Abraha). Even were that date to be known, Muḥammad’s birth is said
2 CONC  to have taken place ‘fifty days or two months after the departure of the elephant, or again ten, fifteen or even twenty years later’ (Lammens, 212, quoting an anonymous Sīra manuscript in the Bibliothèque nationale in Paris). Fur
2 CONC thermore, the reported age of the Prophet was based not on memory of the actual event of his birth but on retrospective calculations from the date of the hijra to Medina (see for example Ibn ʿAsākir, 1/21, which implies that the Y
2 CONC ear of the Elephant should be dated at 552 CE; see also Ibn Hishām, 1/167–168).
2 CONT
2 CONT While she was pregnant, Āmina is reported to have had a dream in which she saw a light shining forth from her, by which she could see the castles of Buṣrā (Bostra) in Syria (al-Yaʿqūbī, 1/361). It is also reported that she was tol
2 CONC d in a dream: ‘you are carrying the prophet of this nation’ (Ibn Saʿd, 1/98; Ibn al-Athīr, ¶ 1/458–459; al-Majlisī, 15/325). According to most reports, ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib went on a trading journey to Syria, and on the 
2 CONC return journey he stayed with his grandmother’s family in Yathrib (Medina). There he fell ill and died before his son was born. However there are also some reports that state that he died either two, seven or even twenty-eight mon
2 CONC ths after Muḥammad’s birth. When he was born, Āmina sent word to his grandfather, ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, who took him to the Kaʿba and prayed over him. According to Ibn Hishām (1/168–169), when the baby was brought back, Āmina told ʿAb
2 CONC d al-Muṭṭalib that she had been instructed to call him by the name Muḥammad.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muḥammad was then brought up by his mother and a devoted servant, Baraka, apart from a period of just over two years when, as was customary, he was sent to be wet-nursed by a woman called Ḥalīma bint Abī Dhuʿayb from a Bedouin tri
2 CONC be, the Banū Saʿd b. Bakr. When Muḥammad was six, or possibly four, years old, Āmina took him to visit her family in Yathrib, but just after they had set out on the return to Mecca, she fell ill at a place called al-Abwāʾ, where s
2 CONC he died and was buried. She is said to have been just thirty years old (Ibn Saʿd, 1/99; al-Yaʿqūbī, 2/10; al-Majlisī, 15/115; Āyatī, 52; Lings, 23–27).
2 CONT
2 CONT Many scholars, especially the Shiʿis, hold that Āmina was a monotheist, believing that both of the Prophet’s parents and all his forefathers were of the monotheistic faith of Abraham (millat Ibrāhīm) (al-Majlisī, 15/11–118; cf. Ib
2 CONC n Saʿd, 1/116–117). When the Prophet passed by al-Abwāʾ at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyya, he made a special visit to his mother’s grave to tend it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Muhammad Ali Mowlavi Tr. Farzin Negahban Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Āyatī, Muḥammad Ibrāhīm, Tārīkh-i payāmbar-i Islām, ed. Abū al-Qāsim Gurjī (Tehran, 1362 Sh./1983)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn ʿAsākir, ʿAlī, Taʾrīkh ¶ Dimashq (Beirut, 1329–1332/1911–1914)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn al-Athīr, al-Kāmil
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn Hishām, ʿAbd al-Malik, al-Sīra al-Nabawiyya (Cairo, 1955)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn Isḥāq, Muḥammad, Sīrat Rasūl Allāh, ed. Ferdinand Wüstenfeld (Göttingen, 1858–1860)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ibn Saʿd, Muḥammad, al-Ṭabaqāt al-kubrā, ed. Iḥsān ʿAbbās (Beirut, n.d.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Lammens, Henri, ‘L’Âge de Mahomet et la chronologie de la sīra’, JA, 10, no. 17 (1911), pp. 209–250
2 CONT
2 CONT Lings, Martin, Muhammad (London, 1986)
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Majlisī, Muḥammad Bāqir, Biḥār al-anwār (Beirut, 1403/1983)
2 CONT
2 CONT Watt, W. Montgomery, ‘Āmina’, EI2, vol. 1, p. 438
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Yaʿqūbī, Aḥmad, Taʾrīkh (Beirut, 1379/1960).
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation Mowlavi, Muhammad Ali; Negahban, Farzin. " Āmina bint Wahb." Encyclopaedia Islamica. Editors-in-Chief: Wilferd Madelung and, Farhad Daftary. Brill Online , 2013. Reference. Jim Harlow. 15 January 2013 <http://referencewor
2 CONC ks.brillonline.com/entries/encyclopaedia-islamica/amina-bint-wahb-COM_0267> ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aminah_bint_Wahb
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C4%80mina_bint_Wahb
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From ''Banu Zahrah'' Tribe.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From ''Banu Zahrah'' Tribe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aminah_bint_Wahb
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aminah was born to Wahb ibn Abd Manaf and Barrah bint ‘Abd al ‘Uzzā ibn ‘Uthmān ibn ‘Abd al-Dār in Mecca.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a member of the Banu Zuhrah clan in the tribe of Quraysh who claimed descent from Ibrahim (Abraham) through his son Ismail (Ishmael). Her ancestor Zuhrah was the elder brother of Qusayy ibn Kilab, who was also an ancestor 
2 CONC of 'Abd Allah ibn Abd al Muttalib. Qusayy ibn Kilab became the first Quraysh custodian of the Ka'aba. Abdul Mutallib, father of 'Abd Allah, fixed the marriage of his youngest son 'Abd Allah with Aminah. She was eventually married 
2 CONC to 'Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Muttalib.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was said that a light shone out of his forehead and that this light was the promise of a Prophet as offspring. Countless woman of Arabia approached 'Abd Allah, who, according to several traditions, was a handsome man; so that t
2 CONC hey might gain the honour of producing the offspring. The light was believed to be transferred to Aminah through 'Abd Allah
2 CONT --------------------
