1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Hisham I or Hisham Al-Reda''' (Arabic: هشام بن عبد الرحمن الداخل‎) was the second Umayyad Emir of Cordoba, ruling from 788 to 796 in the Al-Andalus (Moorish Iberia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Hisham was born in Cordoba. He was the 1st son of Abd ar-Rahman I and his wife, Halul and the younger half brother of Suleiman. He built many mosques and completed the Mezquita. In 792 he called for a jihad. Muslims arrived from a
2 CONC broad. The campaign took place in 793 against the Kingdom of Asturias and the County of Toulouse; in this second campaign he was defeated at Orange by William of Gellone, the Frankish count of Toulouse and first cousin to Charles 
2 CONC the Great. [edit] Death and Assessment
2 CONT
2 CONT Hisham died in 796 C.E. after rule of eight years. He was only forty years old at the time of his death. He was a prototype of Umar II, and strove to establish the islamic way of life. He lived a simple life and avoided regal show
2 CONC  and ostentation. He was a God-fearing man and was known for his impartial justice and sound administration.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hishâm I ibn 'Abd al-Rahmân, amir al-Qurtubi'''
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 756, d. 796
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	'Abd al-Rahmân I al-Dâkhil ibn Mu'âwiya, amir al-Qurtubi1,2 b. 731, d. 788
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Khazraf (?)1 b. circa 736
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the Umayyad. Hishâm I ibn 'Abd al-Rahmân, amir al-Qurtubi was named in full: Hisham bin `Abdul-Rahman Al-Dakhil.2 He was born in 756.2 He was the son of 'Abd al-Rahmân I al-Dâkhil ibn Mu'âwiya, amir al-Qurtubi and Khazraf (?).1
2 CONC ,2 Hishâm I ibn 'Abd al-Rahmân, amir al-Qurtubi succeeded his father to the emirate in 788. He undertook summer campaigns against the kingdom of Asturias after 788.3 He was a witness where Bermudo Iº Fruelez "el Diácono", rey de A
2 CONC sturias contended with summer campaigns against his kingdom by Hishâm I of Córdoba between 788 and 791.3 Emir of Córdoba at Al-Andalus, Spain, between 788 and 796.2,4,3 Hishâm I ibn 'Abd al-Rahmân, amir al-Qurtubi built several mo
2 CONC sques and finished the building of the Mosque of Cordoba.2 He died in 796 at age 40 years.1,2 He died in 799 at age 43 years.4
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi+ b. 771, d. 8221,2
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S187] Royal Genealogy Database, online http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/public/genealogy/
2 CONT
2 CONT [S469] Al-Islam.com, online http://www.al-islam.com/
