1 OCCU 3º emir independiente de Córdoba, desde el 17 de abril de 796 hasta su muerte.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Hakam_I
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Hakam Ibn Hisham Ibn Abd-ar-Rahman I (Arabic: الحكم بن هشام‎) was Umayyad Emir of Cordoba from 796 until 822 in the Al-Andalus (Moorish Iberia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Hakam was the second son of his father, his older brother having died at an early age. When he came to power, he was challenged by his uncles Sulayman and Abdallah, sons of Abd ar-Rahman I. Abdallah took his two sons Ubayd Alla
2 CONC h and Abd al-Malik to the court of Charlemagne in Aix-la-Chapelle to negotiate for aid. In the mean time Sulayman attacked Cordoba, but was defeated and driven back to Merida where he was captured and executed. Abdallah was pardon
2 CONC ed, but was forced to stay in Valencia. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Hakam spent much of his reign suppressing rebellions in Toledo, Saragossa and Merida. The uprisings twice reached Cordoba. In 805 an attempt was made to dethrone Al-Hakam and replace him with his cousin Mohammed ibn al-Kasim. W
2 CONC hen the plot was discovered 72 nobles were captured, crucified and displayed along the banks of the river Guadalquivir. In 818 he crushed a rebellion led by clerics in the suburb of al-Ribad on the south bank of the Guadalquivir r
2 CONC iver. Some 300 notables were captured and crucified, while the rest of the inhabitants were exiled. Some moved to Alexandria in Egypt, some to Fez and Crete. Others joined the Levantine pirates. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Hakam I died in 822 after having ruled for 26 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 771, d. 822
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Hishâm I ibn 'Abd al-Rahmân, amir al-Qurtubi1,2 b. 756, d. 796
2 CONT
2 CONT       Al-Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi was named in full: Al-Hakam bin Hisham bin `Abdul-Rahman Al-Dakhil.2 Of the Umayyad. He was born in 771.2 He was the son of Hishâm I ibn 'Abd al-Rahmân, amir al-Qurtubi.1,2 Al-
2 CONC Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi married Halawah (?) before 792.1 Al-Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi was troubled by dissidence on the part of the Neo-Muslim majority after 796.3 Emir of Córdoba at Al-Anda
2 CONC lus, Spain, between 796 and 822.4,3 He wiped out a pocket of Neo-Muslim resistance by engineering a banquet of Neo-Muslim dignitaries who severed heads landed in the moat ("Day of the Ditch") in 797 at Toledo.3 He was known for hi
2 CONC s firmness.2 He suppressed the Rabadi Revolution.2 He was interested in literature and science, and was also an eloquent poet and preacher.2 He unleashed his troops in the Suburb, to quell the so-called Revolt of Suburb, referring
2 CONC  to an artisan district of Córdoba, where a plot to dethrone the emir was concocted by fanatical religious leaders, and razed the entire district in 805 at Córdoba.3 He died in 822 at age 51 years.1,2
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Halawah (?) b. circa 776
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Abd al-Rahmân II al-Mutawassit ibn al-Hakam, amir al-Qurtubi+ b. 792, d. 22 Sep 8521,2
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S187] Royal Genealogy Database, online http://www.dcs.hull.ac.uk/public/genealogy/
2 CONT
2 CONT [S469] Al-Islam.com, online http://www.al-islam.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT [S515] Thomas F. Glick, Islamic and Christian Spain, pg. 39.
2 CONT
2 CONT [S653] PoH, online http://www.friesian.com/
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Medlands'''
2 CONT
2 CONT al-HAKAM, son of HISHAM I Emir of Córdoba ([770/75]-822).  Abd el-Wahid Merrakechi records "…Hakam b. Hicham b. Mohammed b. Abd er-Rahman ed-Dakhil…" when reciting the ancestry of his descendant "El-Motadd billah" Emir of Córdoba[
2 CONC 63].  In an earlier passage, Abd el-Wahid Merrakechi names El-Hakam as son of Hisham and "une concubine…Zokhrouf", adding that he was about 22 years old when he succeeded his father and was "un homme impie et débauché"[64].  He su
2 CONC cceeded his father 796 as al-HAKAM I Emir of Córdoba.  Distracted by the challenges to the succession by his uncles Sulayman and Abd-Allah, attacks on the Christians decreased at the start of his reign.  However, by 810 the attack
2 CONC s intensified again and in 816 Emir al-Hakam launched a major offensive against the kingdom of Asturias[65].  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Alhacam” reigned 26 years and six months in Córdoba, adding in a later paragraph 
2 CONC that he was the son of “Iscem”[66]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT al-Hakam had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # ABD al-RAHMAN ([792]-23 Sep 852).  Abd el-Wahid Merrakechi records "…Abd er-Rahman b. Hakam b. Hicham b. Mohammed b. Abd er-Rahman ed-Dakhil…" when reciting the ancestry of his descendant "El-Motadd billah" Emir of Córdoba[67]. 
2 CONC  He succeeded his father 822 as ABD al-RAHMAN II Emir of Córdoba.  During his reign, the court became more formal, the administration of government more organised (following eastern Islamic models), and the respect of Islamic trad
2 CONC ition imposed more strictly.  He also encouraged scholars and poets, and constructed mosques in Seville and Jaen, as well as an extension to the mosque in Córdoba.  He founded the city of Murcia in 831.  In Summer 844, Viking wars
2 CONC hips attacked Seville, but the invaders were effectively beaten by the Emir's troops after they landed.  The Chronicon Albeldense records that “Abderahaman” reigned 32 years and six months in Córdoba while Ordoño I was king of Ast
2 CONC urias, adding in a later paragraph that he was the son of “Haccam”[68].  Abd al-Rahman II had five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT #  al-MUGHIRA
2 CONT #  SAID
2 CONT # UMAYYA
2 CONT # al-WALID bin al-HAKAM .  He led an army to attack Galicia in 838[78].   
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Al Hakam I de Córdoba (b. 770, d. 21 May 822)
2 CONT
2 CONT Al Hakam I de Córdoba (son of Hisham I de Córdoba) was born 770, and died 21 May 822. He married Adjab.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Notes for Al Hakam I de Córdoba: BIOGRAPHY: Subiu ao trono com 26 anos.
2 CONT
2 CONT De pele morena, alto e delgado, era cruel e impiedoso.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preocupou-se, todavia, com a cultura dos seus súbditos mandando construir diversas escolas. Mandou vir, para o efeito, professores do Oriente.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Al Hakam I de Córdoba and Adjab are: +Abd Al Rahman II De Córdoba, b. 792, d. 22 Sep 852.
