1 CONT
1 CONT Most Muslims, and Western academics who trust Islamic traditions, accept a much more detailed version of Muhammad's life.
1 CONT
1 CONT Before Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Muhammad before Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT Genealogy
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Family tree of Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad traced his genealogy as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad was born into the Quraysh tribe. He is the son of Abd Allah, who is son of Abd al-Muttalib (Shaiba) son of Hashim (Amr) ibn Abd Manaf (al-Mughira) son of Qusai (Zaid) ibn Kilab ibn Murra son of Ka'b ibn Lu'ay son of Ghali
1 CONC b ibn Fahr (Quraish) son of Malik ibn an-Nadr (Qais) the son of Kinana son of Khuzaimah son of Mudrikah (Amir) son of Ilyas son of Mudar son of Nizar son of Ma'ad ibn Adnan, whom the northern Arabs believed to be their common ance
1 CONC stor. Adnan in turn is said to have been a descendant of Ishmael, son of Abraham. (ibn means "son of" in Arabic; alternate names of people with two names are given in parentheses.)[19]
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also called Abu-Qaasim (meaning "father of Qaasim") by some, after his short-lived first son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Childhood
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad was born into an affluent family settled in the northern Arabian town of Mecca. Tradition places it in the Year of the Elephant, commonly identified with 570. Some[citation needed] calculate his birthday as 20 April of th
1 CONC at year, while Shi'a Muslims believe it to have been 26 April 570. Other sources calculate the year of his birth to have been 571. Muhammad's father, Abdullah, had died almost six months before he was born and the young boy was br
1 CONC ought up by his paternal grandfather Abd al-Muttalib, of the Banu Hashim clan of the Quraysh tribe. At the age of six, Muhammad lost his mother Amina and became fully orphaned. "Many years later, when he was exiled by his Meccan o
1 CONC pponents, on his first pilgrimage from Medina to Mecca, he stopped at his mother's grave and cried bitterly, bringing tears to the eyes of his companions."[20] When he was eight years of age, his grandfather Abd al-Muttalib, who h
1 CONC ad become his guardian, also died. Muhammad now came under the care of his uncle Abu Talib, the new leader of the Hashim clan of the Quraish tribe, the most powerful in Mecca.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mecca was a thriving commercial center, due in great part to a shrine (now called the Kaaba) that housed statues of many Arabian gods. [citation needed] Merchants from various tribes would visit Mecca during the pilgrimage season,
1 CONC  when all inter-tribal warfare was forbidden and they could trade in safety. While still in his teens, Muhammad began accompanying his uncle on trading journeys to Syria. He thus became well-travelled and knowledgeable about forei
1 CONC gn ways.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT Middle years
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad became a merchant. He "was involved in trade between the Indian ocean and the Mediterranean Sea."[21] He gained a reputation for reliability and honesty that attracted a proposal from Khadijah, a forty-year-old widow in 5
1 CONC 95.[21] Muhammad consented to the marriage, which by all accounts was a happy one.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ibn Ishaq records that Khadijah bore Muhammad six children: three sons named Al Qasem, Abdullah (who is also called Al Tayeb and Al Taher) and Ibrahim, and four daughters. He was also called Abul Qasim (father of Qasim) after his 
1 CONC eldest son Qasim, according to Arab customs. All of Khadija's children were born before Muhammad reported receiving his first revelation. His son Qasim died at the age of two. The four daughters are said to be Zainab, Ruqayyah, Um
1 CONC m Kulthum, and Fatima.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Shi'a say that Muhammad had only the one daughter, Fatima, and that the other daughters were either children of Khadijah by her previous marriage, or children of her sister.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Beginnings of the Qur'an
1 CONT The mountain of Hira where, according to Muslim tradition, Muhammad received his first revelation.
1 CONT The mountain of Hira where, according to Muslim tradition, Muhammad received his first revelation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad often retreated to Mount Hira near Mecca. Islamic tradition holds that the angel Gabriel began communicating with him here in the year 610 and commanded Muhammad to recite the following verses:[22]
1 CONT
1 CONT         Proclaim! (or read!) in the name of thy Lord and Cherisher, Who created- Created man, out of a (mere) clot of congealed blood: Proclaim! And thy Lord is Most Bountiful,- He Who taught (the use of) the pen,- Taught man that
1 CONC  which he knew not.[23]
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon receiving the first revelation he was scared, and when he returned home he related the event to his wife Khadijah. He was consoled and reassured by Khadijah and her Christian cousin, Waraqah ibn Nawfal. Waraqah was immediatel
1 CONC y enthusiastic, but Khadijah proceeded more cautiously, and was only satisfied that the revelations had indeed come from a good source after the conclusion of a test she had devised to determine that very thing. This was followed 
1 CONC by a pause of three years during which Muhammad had gave himself up further to prayers and spiritual practices. When the revelations resumed he was reassured and commanded to begin preaching.[24][25]
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Welch, the revelations were accompanied by mysterious seizures.[9] Muhammad was confident that he could distinguish his own thoughts from these messages.[26]
1 CONT
1 CONT Rejection
1 CONT
1 CONT His wife Khadijah and Waraqah, were the first to believe that Muhammad was a prophet. They were soon followed by Muhammad's ten-year-old cousin Ali ibn Abi Talib, close friend Abu Bakr and adopted son Zaid bin Muhammad (later know
1 CONC n as Zaid bin Haarith.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 613, Muhammad began to preach amongst Meccans most of whom ignored it and a few mocked him, while some others became his followers. There were three main groups of early converts to Islam: younger brothers and sons of great
1 CONC  merchants; people who had fallen out of the first rank in their tribe or failed to attain it; and the weak, mostly unprotected foreigners.[27]
1 CONT
1 CONT As the ranks of Muhammad's followers swelled, he became a threat to the local tribes and the rulers of the city, whose wealth rested upon the Kaaba, the focal point of Meccan religious life, which Muhammad threatened to overthrow.
1 CONC  Muhammad’s denunciation of the Meccan traditional religion was especially offensive to his own tribe, the Quraysh, as they were the guardians of the Ka'aba. The great merchants tried to come to some arrangements with Muhammad in 
1 CONC exchange for abandoning his preaching. They offered him admission into the inner circle of merchants and establishing his position in the circle by an advantageous marriage. [28] Some of Muhammad's followers fled to the Ethiopian 
1 CONC Kingdom of Aksum and founded a small colony there under the protection of the Christian Ethiopian king. (See Islam in Ethiopia).
1 CONT
1 CONT Several suras and parts of suras are said to date from this time, and reflect its circumstances: see for example al-Masadd, al-Humaza, parts of Maryam and al-Anbiya, al-Kafirun, and Abasa.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 619, the "year of sorrows", both Muhammad's wife Khadijah and his uncle Abu Talib died. The relationship between Muhammad's group of followers and Muhammad's own Quraysh clan, which were already bad, worsened still further.[29]
1 CONC  The controversial Satanic verses incident, if it happened, happened at this time. [30]
1 CONT
1 CONT Isra and Miraj
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Isra and Mi'raj
1 CONT
1 CONT A 16th century Persian miniature painting celebrating Muhammad's ascent into the Heavens, a journey known as the Miraj. Muhammad's face is veiled, a common practice in Islamic art.
1 CONT A 16th century Persian miniature painting celebrating Muhammad's ascent into the Heavens, a journey known as the Miraj. Muhammad's face is veiled, a common practice in Islamic art.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some time in 620, Muhammad told his followers that he had experienced the Isra and Miraj, a miraculous journey said to have been accomplished in one night along with Angel Gabriel. In the first part of the journey, the Isra, he is
1 CONC  said to have travelled from Mecca to "the farthest mosque" (in Arabic: masjid al-aqsa), which Muslims usually identify with the Temple Mount in Jerusalem. In the second part, the Miraj, Muhammad is said to have toured heaven and 
1 CONC hell, and spoken with earlier prophets, such as Abraham, Moses, and Jesus. Ibn Ishaq, author of first biography of Muhammad, presents this event as a spiritual experience while later historians like Al-Tabari and Ibn Kathir presen
1 CONC t it as a physical journey.[31] Those Muslims subscribing to the latter view consider the place under the Dome of the Rock the site from which Muhammad ascended to heaven.
1 CONT Timeline of Muhammad
1 CONT Important dates and locations in the life of Muhammad
1 CONT c. 569 Death of his father, `Abd Allah
1 CONT c. 570 Possible date of birth, April 20: Mecca
1 CONT 570 Legendary unsuccessful Ethiopian attack on Mecca
1 CONT 576 Death of Mother
1 CONT 578 Death of Grandfather
1 CONT c. 583 Takes trading journeys to Syria
1 CONT c. 595 Meets and marries Khadijah
1 CONT 610 First reports of Qur'anic revelation: Mecca
1 CONT c. 610 Appears as Prophet of Islam: Mecca
1 CONT c. 613 Begins spreading message of Islam publicly: Mecca
1 CONT c. 614 Begins to gather following: Mecca
1 CONT c. 615 Emigration of Muslims to Ethiopia
1 CONT 616 Banu Hashim clan boycott begins
1 CONT c. 618 Medinan Civil War: Medina
1 CONT 619 Banu Hashim clan boycott ends
1 CONT 619 The year of sorrows: Khadijah and Abu Talib die
1 CONT c. 620 Isra and Miraj
1 CONT 622 Emigrates to Medina (Hijra)
1 CONT 624 Battle of Badr Muslims defeat Meccans
1 CONT 624 Expulsion of Banu Qaynuqa
1 CONT 625 Battle of Uhud Meccans battle Muslims
1 CONT 625 Expulsion of Banu Nadir
1 CONT 626 Attack on Dumat al-Jandal: Syria
1 CONT 627 Battle of the Trench
1 CONT 627 Destruction of Banu Qurayza
1 CONT 627 Bani Kalb subjugation: Dumat al-Jandal
1 CONT 628 Treaty of Hudaybiyya
1 CONT c. 628 Gains access to Mecca shrine Kaaba
1 CONT 628 Conquest of the Khaybar oasis
1 CONT 629 First hajj pilgrimage
1 CONT 629 Attack on Byzantine empire fails: Battle of Mu'tah
1 CONT 630 Attacks and bloodlessly captures Mecca
1 CONT c. 630 Battle of Hunayn
1 CONT c. 630 Siege of Taif
1 CONT 630 Establishes theocracy: Conquest of Mecca
1 CONT c. 631 Rules most of the Arabian peninsula
1 CONT c. 632 Attacks the Ghassanids: Tabuk
1 CONT 632 Farewell hajj pilgrimage
1 CONT 632 Death (June 8): Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT Hijra to Ethiopia
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Migration to Abyssinia
1 CONT
1 CONT In 615 AD/CE, a band of Muslims were counseled by the Prophet Muhammad to escape persecution in Mecca and travel to Ethiopia, which was ruled by a Christian king. (see Islam in Ethiopia) In that year, his followers were fleeing fr
1 CONC om Mecca's leading tribe, the Quraish, who sent emissaries to bring them back to Arabia, but the King of Ethiopia protected Muhammad's followers. Since then, Muhammad himself instructed his followers who came to Ethiopia, to respe
1 CONC ct and protect Ethiopia as well as live in peace with Ethiopian Christians. Accordingly, some scholars state that Ethiopia was the country that saved Islam from its near destruction and termination.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad in Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT Hijra to Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Migration to Medina
1 CONT     Main article: Muhammad in Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT By 622, Muhammad then emigrated to Medina, then known as Yathrib, a large agricultural oasis where there were a number of Muslim converts. By breaking the link with his own tribe, Muhammad demonstrated that tribal and family loyal
1 CONC ties were insignificant compared to the bonds of Islam, a revolutionary idea in the tribal society of Arabia. This Hijra or emigration (traditionally translated into English as "flight") marks the beginning of the Islamic calendar
1 CONC . The Muslim calendar counts dates from the Hijra, which is why Muslim dates have the suffix AH (After Hijra).
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad came to Medina as a mediator, invited to resolve the feud between the Arab factions of Aws and Khazraj. He ultimately did so by absorbing both factions into his Muslim community, forbidding bloodshed among Muslims. Howeve
1 CONC r, Medina was also home to a number of Jewish tribes, divided into three major clans: Banu Qainuqa, Banu Qurayza and Banu Nadir, and some minor groups.[32]
1 CONT
1 CONT There was fighting in Yathrib for around a hundred years before 620. The Jewish tribes allied with other clans and were sometimes on opposing sides.[32] The recurring slaughters and disagreements over the resulting claims, especia
1 CONC lly after the great battle of Bu'ath in which all the clans were involved, made it obvious to them that the tribal conceptions of blood-feud and an eye for an eye were no longer workable unless "there was one man with authority to
1 CONC  adjudicate in disputed cases."[32] A delegation from Medina, consisting of the representatives of the twelve important clans of Medina, invited Muhammad as a neutral outsider to Medina to serve as the chief arbitrator for the ent
1 CONC ire community.[32][33] Among the things Muhammad did in order to settle down the longstanding grievances among the tribes of Medina was drafting a document known as the Constitution of Medina (date debated), "establishing a kind o
1 CONC f alliance or federation" among the eight Medinan tribes and Muslim emigrants from Mecca, which specified the rights and duties of all citizens and the relationship of the different communities in Medina (including that of the Mus
1 CONC lim community to other communities specifically the Jews and other "Peoples of the Book").[32][33]
1 CONT
1 CONT Beginnings of conflict
1 CONT
1 CONT Relations between Mecca and Medina rapidly worsened (see surat al-Baqara). Meccans confiscated all the property that the Muslims had left in Mecca.[citation needed] In Medina, Muhammad signed treaties of alliance and mutual help w
1 CONC ith neighboring tribes. Mecca declared its hostility and status of war with the Muslims.
1 CONT
1 CONT In March of 624, Muhammad led some three hundred warriors in a raid on a Meccan merchant caravan. The Meccans successfully defended the caravan, but then decided to teach the Muslims a lesson and marched against Medina. It should 
1 CONC be noted that Islamic scholars question narratives regarding looting the caravan on the basis of the Qur'anic version of the account.[34][opinion needs balancing] On March 15, 624 near a place called Badr, the Meccans and the Musl
1 CONC ims clashed. Though outnumbered more than three times (one thousand to three hundred - majority of Muslim historians put the exact total at 313) in the battle, the Muslims met with success, killing at least forty-five Meccans and 
1 CONC taking seventy prisoners for ransom; only fourteen Muslims died.[35] This marked the real beginning of Muslim military battles.
1 CONT
1 CONT To his followers, the victory in Badr appeared to be divine authentication of Muhammad's prophethood. Muhammad and his followers were now a dominant force in the oasis of Yathrib (Medina).
1 CONT
1 CONT After Khadija's death, Muhammad married Aisha, the daughter of his friend Abu Bakr (who would later emerge as the first leader of the Muslims after Muhammad's death). In Medina, he married Hafsah, daughter of Umar (who would event
1 CONC ually become Abu Bakr's successor).
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad's daughter Fatima married Ali, Muhammad's cousin. According to the Sunni, another daughter, Umm Kulthum, married Uthman. Each of these men, in later years, would emerge as successors to Muhammad and political leaders of t
1 CONC he Muslims. Thus, all four caliphs were linked to Muhammad by marriage. Sunni Muslims regard these caliphs as the Rashidun, or Rightly Guided. (See Succession to Muhammad for more information on the controversy on the succession t
1 CONC o the caliphate).
1 CONT
1 CONT The conflict with Mecca
1 CONT
1 CONT In 625 the Meccan leader Abu Sufyan marched on Medina with three thousand men. Urged on by younger Muslims fired up by the victory at Badr and against the advice of Abdallah ibn Ubayy to last out the attack inside the town, Muhamm
1 CONC ad led his force outside and fought the Battle of Uhud on March 23, that ended in a Muslim defeat (According to Watt however it was not a Muslim defeat from a military standpoint. The Meccans, thinking themselves of having Arabia 
1 CONC under their control, had aimed to destroy Muslims completely. But they completely failed to achieve this aim. They killed 75 Muslims for the loss of 77 of their own in Badr. [36]) However, the Meccan did not occupy the town and wi
1 CONC thdrew to Mecca because he could not attack on Muhammad's position again for military loss, low morale and possibility of Muslim resistance in the town. There was also hope that Abd-Allah ibn Ubayy leading a group of Muslims in Me
1 CONC dina could be win over by diplomacy. [37] In April 627, Abu Sufyan led another strong force against Medina, but couldn't overcome the defenders in the Battle of the Trench.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the Muslims' victory at the Battle of the Trench, the Muslims were able, through conversion and conquest, to extend their rule to many of the neighboring cities and tribes.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad and the Jewish tribes of Medina
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Muhammad and the Jews
1 CONT
1 CONT Many Medinans converted to the faith of the Meccan immigrants, but the Jewish tribes did not. Much to Muhammad's disappointment, they rejected his claim to be prophet.[33] Their opposition "may well have been for political as well
1 CONC  as religious reasons".[38]On religious grounds, the Jews were skeptical of the possibility of a non-Jewish prophet,[39] and also had concerns about possible incompatibilities between the Qur'an and their own scriptures.[39][40] T
1 CONC he Qur'an's response regarding the possibility of a non-Jew being a prophet was that Abraham was not a Jew. The Qur'an also claimed that it was "restoring the pure monotheism of Abraham which had been corrupted in various, clearly
1 CONC  specified, ways by Jews and Christians".[39]
1 CONT
1 CONT After each major battle with the Medinans, Muhammad attacked one of the Jewish tribes (see 2:100). After Badr and Uhud, the Banu Qainuqa and Banu Nadir, respectively, were expelled "with their families and possessions" from Medina
1 CONC . After the Battle of the Trench in 627, the Muslims accused the Jews of Banu Qurayza of conspiring with the Meccans, then wiped them out.[41]
1 CONT
1 CONT Watt states that the Qurayza did not commit any hostile act,[42] and that they were probably negotiating with Mecca.[42] After the Qurayza had succumbed to a siege, several members of the Aws interceded on behalf of their old alli
1 CONC es. Muhammad agreed to appoint one of their chiefs as a judge, who decided that the Qurayza men should be beheaded, the women and children enslaved, and their properties confiscated. The Muslims exacted this punishment.[43] Howeve
1 CONC r, there are some scholarly views that challenge this interpretation of events, citing concerns about the reliability of certain early historical sources.[44]
1 CONT
1 CONT The truce of Hudaybiyya
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Treaty of Hudaybiyya
1 CONT
1 CONT Although Muhammad had already delivered verses (2:196-2:210) about the performing of Hajj, Muhammad and Muslims did not perform it due to the enmity of the Quraish. It was the month of Shawwal 6 A.H. when Muhammad saw in a vision 
1 CONC that he was shaving his head after the Hajj.[45][46] Muhammad therefore decided to perform the Hajj in the following month. Hence around the 13th of March, 628 with 1400 Companions he went towards Mecca without the least intention
1 CONC  of giving battle.[47] But the Quraish were determined to offer resistance to Muslims and they posted themselves outside Mecca, closing all access to the city.[47] In order to settle the dispute peacefully, Muhammad halted at a pl
1 CONC ace called Hudaybiyya. Hence after series of talks a treaty was signed. The main points of treaty were the following:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. The two parties and their allies should desist from hostilities against each other[48][49]
1 CONT    2. Muhammad, should not perform Hajj this year[48][50]
1 CONT    3. They may come next year to perform Hajj (unarmed) but shall not stay in Mecca for more than three days[48][50]
1 CONT    4. Any Muslim living in Mecca cannot settle in Medina, but Medinan Muslims may come and join Meccans (and will not be returned).[51]
1 CONT
1 CONT Many Muslims were not satisfied with the terms of the treaty. However, the Qur'anic sura "Al-Fath" (The Victory) 48:1-48:29 assured the Muslims that the expedition from which they were now returning must be considered a victorious
1 CONC  one.[52][53] The Muslims did benefit following the treaty; the men of Mecca and Medina could now meet in peace and discuss Islam. Hence, during the following two years the community of Islam more than doubled.[54][55][56]
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad's letters to the Heads of State
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Muslim tradition, after the signing of the truce, Muhammad sent letters to many rulers of the world, asking them to convert to Islam.[57][58][59] Hence he sent messengers (with letters) to Heraclius of the Byzantine E
1 CONC mpire (the eastern Roman Empire), Chosroes of Persia, the chief of Yemen and to some others.[57][58]
1 CONT
1 CONT Conquest of Mecca
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Muhammad after the conquest of Mecca
1 CONT
1 CONT The Kaaba in Mecca held a major economic and religious role for the area, it became the Muslim Qibla, or direction for Salat
1 CONT The Kaaba in Mecca held a major economic and religious role for the area, it became the Muslim Qibla, or direction for Salat
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Conquest of Mecca
1 CONT
1 CONT The truce of Hudaybiyya had been in force for two years.[60][61] The tribe of Khuz'aah had a friendly relationship with Muhammad, while on the other hand their enemies, the Banu Bakr, had an alliance with the Meccans.[60][62] A cl
1 CONC an of the Bakr made a night raid against the Khuz'aah, killing a few of them.[60][62] The Meccans helped their allies (i.e., the Banu Bakr) with weapons and, according to some sources, a few Meccans also took part in the fighting.
1 CONC [60] After this event, Muhammad sent a message to Mecca with three conditions, asking them to accept one of them. These were the following[63]
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. The Meccans were to pay blood-money for those slain among the Khuza'ah tribe, or
1 CONT    2. They should have nothing to do with the Banu Bakr, or
1 CONT    3. They should declare the truce of Hudaybiyya null.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Meccans replied that they would accept only the third condition.[63] However, soon they realized their mistake and sent Abu Safyan to renew the Hudaybiyya treaty, but now his request was declined by Muhammad. Muhammad began to
1 CONC  prepare for a campaign.[64]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 630, Muhammad marched on Mecca with an enormous force, said to number more than ten thousand men. Most Meccans converted to Islam, and Muhammad subsequently destroyed all of the statues of Arabian gods in and around the Kaaba, 
1 CONC without any exception. Henceforth the pilgrimage would be a Muslim pilgrimage and the shrine was converted to a Muslim shrine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Unification of Arabia
1 CONT
1 CONT The capitulation of Mecca and the defeat of an alliance of enemy tribes at Hunayn effectively brought the greater part of the Arabian peninsula under Muhammad's authority. However, this authority was not enforced by a regular gove
1 CONC rnment, as Muhammad chose instead to rule through personal relationships and tribal treaties. The Muslims were clearly the dominant force in Arabia, and most of the remaining tribes and states hastened to convert to Islam.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT The Al-Masjid al-Nabawi is Islam's second most sacred site; the Green dome in the background stands above Muhammad's tomb
1 CONT The Al-Masjid al-Nabawi is Islam's second most sacred site; the Green dome in the background stands above Muhammad's tomb
1 CONT
1 CONT In 632 Muhammad fell ill and suffered for several days with head pain and weakness. He succumbed on Monday, June 8, 632, in the city of Medina, at the age of sixty-three. He is buried in his tomb (what was his house) adjacent to M
1 CONC osque of the Prophet in Medina.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad as a military leader
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Muhammad as a general
1 CONT
1 CONT For most of the sixty-three years of his life, Muhammad was a merchant, then a religious leader. He took up the sword late in his life. He was an active military leader for ten years.
1 CONT This section is a stub. You can help by expanding it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family life
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Muhammad's marriages
1 CONT
1 CONT This article or section does not cite its references or sources.
1 CONT Please help improve this article by introducing appropriate citations. (help, get involved!)
1 CONT This article has been tagged since January 2007.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad's life is traditionally defined into two epochs: pre-hijra (emigration) in Mecca, a city in northern Arabia, from the year 570 to 622 , and post-hijra in Medina, from 622 until his death in 632. All but two of his marriag
1 CONC es were contracted after the migration to Medina.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married 11 or 13 women depending upon the differing accounts of who his wives were. At the age of 25, Muhammad married Khadijah which lasted for 25 years.[65] This marriage is described as "long" and "happy," and he relied upon
1 CONC  Khadija in many ways.[66][67] After her death, friends of Muhammad advised him to marry again, but he was reluctant to do so.[67][68] It was suggested to Muhammad by Khawla bint Hakim, that he should marry Sawda bint Zama, a Musl
1 CONC im widow, or Aisha. Muhammad is said to have asked her to arrange for him to marry both. Later, Muhammad married additional wives, to make for a total of eleven, of whom nine or ten survived him. Scholars such as Esposito and Watt
1 CONC  hold that most of the marriages had political or social motives.[69][70]
1 CONT
1 CONT The status of several of Muhammad's wives is disputed by scholars. Maria al-Qibtiyya may have been a slave, a freed slave, or a wife.[citation needed] While there is some debate about the age of Aisha (Ayesha), most references, in
1 CONC cluding the Bukhari Hadith, put the marriage age at 5 or 6 and consummation of the marriage at the age of 9.[71][72][73]
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad had children by only two wives. Khadijah is said to have borne him four daughters and a son; only one daughter, Fatima and her children survived her father, see Shia. Some say that his daughter Zainab, mother to a daughte
1 CONC r called Amma or Umama, survived him as well.[citation needed] Shi'a Muslims dispute the number of Muhammad's children, stating that he had only one daughter, and that the other "daughters" were step-daughters. Maria al-Qibtiyya b
1 CONC ore him a son, but the child died when he was ten months old.
1 CONT
1 CONT Descendants of Muhammad are known as sharifs ???? (plural: ?????? Ashraaf) or sayyid. Many rulers and notables in Muslim countries, past and present have professed such descent, with various degrees of credibility, such as the Fat
1 CONC imid dynasty of North Africa, the Idrisids, the current royal families of Jordan. In various Muslim countries, there are societies of varying credibility that authenticate claims of descent.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT There is some dispute between Shia scholars regarding the genealogy of the four daughters of Khadija on whether they were born to Khadijah from her marriage to Muhammad, an earlier marriage, or if they were in fact the daughters o
1 CONC f a widowed and dead sister of Khadija. Sunni's believe he had four daughters with Khadîjah. Shi'a accept Fatimah to be Muhammad's only surviving child,[citation needed] while some Sunni question that.[citation needed]
1 CONT
1 CONT There is also a difference of opinion regarding whether he had two or four sons. The conflict arises from some reports on the sons of Khadijah mentioning two sons called Tahir and Tayyab,[citation needed] and another mentioning on
1 CONC e called Abdullah who was also called Tahir and possibly also called Tayyab.[citation needed] Ibrâhîm was the only child borne to him by Maria during his residence in Medina and the last to be born. Abdullâh was born after he decl
1 CONC ared himself a prophet but died during his residence in Mecca. All his other sons died before his claims of prophecy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Islamic prayer, Muslims end with the second tashahhud asking God to bless Muhammad and his descendants just as Abraham and his descendants were blessed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Khadijah:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Abd-Allah ibn Muhammad
1 CONT     * Qasim ibn Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT Daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ruqayyah bint Muhammad
1 CONT     * Umm Kulthum bint Muhammad
1 CONT     * Zainab bint Muhammad
1 CONT     * Fatima Zahra
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Maria:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ibrahim ibn Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Companions
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main articles: Sahaba and Salaf
1 CONT
1 CONT The term Sahaba (companion) refers to anyone who meets three criteria: to be a contemporary of Muhammad, to have heard Muhammad speak on at least one occasion, and to be a convert to Islam. Companions are considered the ultimate s
1 CONC ources for the oral traditions, or hadith, on which much of Muslim law and practice are based. The following are a few examples in alphabetic order:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Abdullah ibn Abbas
1 CONT     * Abu Bakr
1 CONT     * Abu Dharr
1 CONT     * Ali ibn Abi Talib
1 CONT     * Ammar
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Bilal
1 CONT     * Hamza
1 CONT     * Al-Miqdad
1 CONT     * Sa'd
1 CONT     * Zayd
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Salman the Persian
1 CONT     * Talha
1 CONT     * Umar
1 CONT     * Uthman
1 CONT     * Zubair
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad the reformer
1 CONT The neutrality of this section is disputed.
1 CONT Please see the discussion on the talk page.
1 CONT
1 CONT     See also: Muhammad the Reformer
1 CONT     Main article: Reforms under Islam (610-661)
1 CONT
1 CONT Bernard Lewis says that there are two important political traditions in Islam - one that views Muhammad as a statesman in Medina, and another that views him as a rebel in Mecca. He sees Islam itself as a type of revolution that gr
1 CONC eatly changed the societies into which the new religion was brought.[74]
1 CONT
1 CONT Historians generally agree that Islamic social reforms in areas such as social security, family structure, and the rights of women improved on what was present in existing Arab society.[74][75][76][77][78]
1 CONT
1 CONT Economic reforms addressed the plight of the poor, which was becoming an issue in pre-Islamic Mecca.[79] The Qur'an requires payment of an alms tax (zakat) for the benefit of the poor,[80] and as Muhammad's position grew in power 
1 CONC he demanded that those tribes who wanted to ally with him implement the zakat in particular.[81]
1 CONT
1 CONT Miracles in the Muslim biographies
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Islamic view of miracles
1 CONT
1 CONT The Dome of the Rock, built atop the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, marks the spot from which Muslims believe Muhammad ascended to Paradise.
1 CONT The Dome of the Rock, built atop the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, marks the spot from which Muslims believe Muhammad ascended to Paradise.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pre-modern Muslim biographies of Muhammad envisions Muhammad as a cosmic figure, invested with superhuman qualities. Modern Muslim biographies of Muhammad however portray him as a progressive social reformer, a political leade
1 CONC r and a model of human virtue. The view of these modern biographies is that Muhammad's real miracle, as Daniel Brown states modern historians would probably agree, 'was not a moon split or a sighing palm tree, but the transformati
1 CONC on of the Arabs from marauding bands of nomads into world conquerors.'[82]
1 CONT
1 CONT Carl Ernst believes that this main shift in the treatment of Muhammad has been a response to the stridently negative depictions of Muhammad created by European authors.[83] Daniel Brown adds two more reasons: First, Muslims in the
1 CONC  eighteenth and nineteenth centuries were faced with social and political turmoil. The desire for the restoration of the Muslim community encouraged them to view Muhammad as a model for social and political reform. And lastly, 'th
1 CONC e ongoing challenge of reforming or reviving Islamic law perpetuated concern for the life of Muhammad as a normative model for human behavior.'[82] Ernst states that this main shift reflects the growth of bourgeois scientific rati
1 CONC onalism in Muslim countries.[14]
1 CONT This section is a stub. You can help by expanding it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Traditional views of Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT Seal of the Prophets
1 CONT The Muslim Profession of faith, the Shahada, illustrates the Muslim conception of the role of Muhammad - "There is No God (?ilah) but God(Allah), and Muhammad is His Messenger." As shown on the Flag of Saudi Arabia
1 CONT The Muslim Profession of faith, the Shahada, illustrates the Muslim conception of the role of Muhammad - "There is No God (?ilah)[84] but God(Allah), and Muhammad is His Messenger." As shown on the Flag of Saudi Arabia
1 CONT
1 CONT Muslims believe Muhammad to be the last in a line of prophets of God (Arabic Allah) and regard his mission as one of restoring the original monotheistic faith of Adam, Abraham and other prophets of Islam that had become corrupted 
1 CONC by man over time.[8][9][10] The Qur'an specifically refers to Muhammad as the "Seal of the Prophets", which is taken by most Muslims to believe him to be the last and greatest of the prophets.[85][14] Scholars such as Welch howeve
1 CONC r hold that this Muslim belief is most likely a later interpretation of the Seal of the Prophets.[9] Carl Ernst considers this phrase to mean that Muhammad's "imprint on history is as final as a wax seal on a letter".[14] Wilferd 
1 CONC Madelung states that the meaning of this term is not certain.[86]
1 CONT
1 CONT More traditions
1 CONT Image made in 1315 of Pre-Prophethood Muhammad re-dedicating the Black Stone at the Kaaba. From Tabriz, Persia and can be found in Rashid al-Dins Jami' al-Tawarikh ("The Universal History" or "Compendium of Chronicles"), held in t
1 CONC he University of Edinburgh.
1 CONT Image made in 1315 of Pre-Prophethood Muhammad re-dedicating the Black Stone at the Kaaba. From Tabriz, Persia and can be found in Rashid al-Dins Jami' al-Tawarikh ("The Universal History" or "Compendium of Chronicles"), held in t
1 CONC he University of Edinburgh.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Muslims tradition narrates miracles during his time growing up in the desert as an infant during the period when Muhammad was placed in the care of a Bedouin wet nurse - Halima Sadia.
1 CONT     * After he returned to Mecca, he is said to have been beloved by all around him because he was such a polite and honest child.
1 CONT     * As a youth, he was called upon to solve a vexing political problem for his Meccan neighbors. They were rebuilding the Kaaba and feuding over which clan should have the honor of raising the Black Stone into place. Muhammad su
1 CONC ggested that the heads of each clan raise the Black Stone on a cloth, so that all had the honor of lifting it. Muhammad then put the stone into its place.
1 CONT     * As a young man and a merchant, Muslim tradition asserts that Muhammad was known to be trustworthy and honest. The other Meccans called him "Al-Amin", the trustworthy one or the honest one.[87] After he proclaimed his prophet
1 CONC hood, however, his neighbors turned against him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Depictions of Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT     Main article: Depictions of Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT This article or section does not cite its references or sources.
1 CONT Please help improve this article by introducing appropriate citations. (help, get involved!)
1 CONT This article has been tagged since January 2007.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muslims differ as to whether or not visual depictions of Muhammad are permissible. The position of the four main Sunni Muslim Maddhabs is that, to prevent idolatry and shirk, visual depictions of Muhammad are forbidden; some non-m
1 CONC addhab groups, such as the Salafi movement, take a similar line.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Shia and others have historically taken a much less restrictive view of such depictions, allowing them if they are meant to praise Muhammad, while a school of Sufi'ism uses calligraphy of the name of Muhammad, Ali, Hussein and
1 CONC  other important people in Muslim History to create images of the people.
1 CONT
1 CONT Muslim veneration of Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT     See also: Muslim veneration for Muhammad, Praise of Muhammad in poetry, Depiction of Muhammad, Islamic music, and Qawwali
1 CONT
1 CONT Muhammad's name, engraved in gold, adorns the walls of Hagia Sophia in Istanbul. Originally a Christian church, it was converted into a mosque after the Fall of Constantinople.
1 CONT Muhammad's name, engraved in gold, adorns the walls of Hagia Sophia in Istanbul. Originally a Christian church, it was converted into a mosque after the Fall of Constantinople.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is traditional for Muslims to illustrate and express love and veneration for Muhammad. This is observed in a number of different ways. When Muslims say or write Muhammad's name, they usually follow it with Peace be upon him or 
1 CONC its Arabic equivalent, sallalahu alayhi wasallam, and for Shias this is extended to Peace be upon him and his descendants. In English this is often abbreviated to "(pbuh)", "(saw)" and "pbuh&hd" for Shias, or even just simply as "
1 CONC p". The Quran gave him the title Apostle of God (Arabic: Rasul-Allah or Rasulallah), which has also been used by Muslims, as well as the title "Prophet". Concerts of Muslim, and especially Sufi, devotional music include songs prai
1 CONC sing Muhammad. There are religious songs Nasheeds which regularly praise Muhammad.
1 CONT
1 CONT Conversely, criticism of Muhammad is often equated with blasphemy, which is punishable by death in Pakistan.[88]
1 CONT
1 CONT Other religious traditions in regard to Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT     * The Druze, who accept most but not all Qur'anic revelations, also consider him a prophet.
1 CONT     * Bahá'ís venerate Muhammad as one of a number of prophets or "Manifestations of God", but consider his teachings to have been superseded by those of Bahá'u'lláh.
1 CONT
